2, and node3\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"postgresql-9.5 installed via apt on all three nodes; pg_lsclusters/systemctl status show 'online' state on each node after install.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e467da3acbf44e67\",\"cmd-d5ef2270da164f82\",\"cmd-ee68aef3ab224b2a\",\"cmd-5c1d8707be994be1\",\"cmd-f66f0415b7cd426e\",\"cmd-a13dda45cfc64a02\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"node1 is configured as the writable primary with replication enabled (wal_level, max_wal_senders, replication role, pg_hba entries)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"postgresql.conf on node1 shows wal_level=hot_standby, listen_addresses='*', max_wal_senders=10; a replicator role with REPLICATION LOGIN was created; pg_hba.conf has host replication entries for node2/node3 IPs; config verified live via 'show wal_level'.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e2341b05cfca491b\",\"cmd-fddd8ceed10b47f3\",\"cmd-f9b31a6c04d149f2\",\"cmd-41e468a4cd6049e5\",\"cmd-c5072e16159f4eed\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"node2 and node3 are configured as read-only standbys streaming from node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"pg_basebackup was run from node1 to seed both standbys' data directories, recovery.conf with standby_mode='on' and primary_conninfo pointing at node1 was set, and pg_lsclusters shows 'online,recovery' on both nodes after startup.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-039efeed850f4a45\",\"cmd-58e318cd7d924c09\",\"cmd-6b2444333b594a48\",\"cmd-caf6a41408774095\",\"cmd-7df3ac32a1b247fd\",\"cmd-7aecbe4ee3054a78\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Streaming replication link between primary and both standbys is actually active\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"pg_stat_replication on node1 lists node2 and node3 both in 'streaming' state; pg_is_in_recovery() returns true on both standbys, confirming an active replication topology, not just static config.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b968e594776d48e3\",\"cmd-d6e59d70766647f6\",\"cmd-95ae9aa595154da4\"]},{\"id\":\"req-5\",\"requirement\":\"Changes committed on the primary are visible on both standbys, and standbys reject writes (read-only)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A repl_test table with rows was created and inserted on node1; the identical rows appeared on both node2 and node3 within ~1s; INSERT attempts on both standbys failed with 'cannot execute INSERT in a read-only transaction', directly demonstrating the read-only/replicated behavior required by the task.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-75ce519365b947f8\",\"cmd-ff26d031efa842bb\",\"cmd-4fa6eee866854bdd\"]},{\"id\":\"req-6\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart protocol demonstrates healthy baseline, restart, changed boot identity, and recovered replication/data state for all three nodes\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"For each node the trace shows: pre-restart boot_id and healthy pg_lsclusters/replication state, a backgrounded 'shutdown -r now' ack, SSH connection-refused confirming the disconnect, reconnection with a changed boot_id (matches fixed after_executor snapshot boot-identity deltas), and post-restart pg_lsclusters/data verification. For node2 and node3, data count and recovery status were confirmed post-restart and node1 confirmed both still streaming. For node1 (primary), post-restart pg_stat_replication shows both standbys reconnected streaming, and a new INSERT after the primary restart was confirmed present on both standbys, directly demonstrating full replication recovery after the primary's own restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-66c370faa3b2469a\",\"cmd-a029d84eae9c424c\",\"cmd-00c87cd81bdc4229\",\"cmd-9d7a34e0a5214481\",\"cmd-cf2f78cabaa249c2\",\"cmd-3f082d686db1451e\",\"cmd-84027d2c96fa4438\",\"cmd-e631396b279c4791\",\"cmd-878807ffc8a1455f\",\"cmd-26b53e32377841c7\",\"cmd-25297c6f84584a28\",\"cmd-f5db4df3296d4c37\",\"cmd-eff82b4beb0c4b60\",\"cmd-472522a79d4e4725\",\"cmd-f328575bf37b4db7\",\"cmd-da378207145d47b9\",\"cmd-699f09d18f5c44e7\",\"cmd-58bf4cb7af6c45b7\",\"cmd-38ca2573e39e4633\",\"cmd-28d9fc72a7b548a3\",\"cmd-9b20b9c053bb4a27\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.95,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.95,\"summary\":\"Reviewed all state-changing commands chronologically. All mutations map to clear task purposes: apt install of postgresql packages (required), postgresql.conf/pg_hba.conf edits and restarts on node1 (required primary config), stop/clear-data-dir/pg_basebackup/recovery.conf on node2 and node3 (required standby seeding), replicator role creation, repl_test table creation/inserts (bounded evidence-collection), and the three sequential node restarts (required by the fixed restart protocol). Two minor troubleshooting steps occurred: an initial pg_basebackup attempt failed because the data directory was non-empty (cmd-9721148416724fae, cmd-f4ac28fe30d848dc), and a 'find -delete' cleanup attempt failed with a permission error (cmd-e7d2f54776fa4f67, cmd-0b61b53a44cd4032) before the executor correctly used 'rm -rf' (cmd-4f97dd9bb6f84d01, cmd-d620550c218d41de) to clear the directories -- these are bounded, low-risk, non-destructive discovery steps directly en route to the required data-dir reset, not unrelated experiments. A temporary replication password file was created on node1 (cmd-8dc7b82c3aee4936) and explicitly removed afterward (cmd-15d4adefc7364b8a), leaving no residue. The after-executor lifecycle snapshot shows no failed services, no unexpected listening-socket exposure, and clean package health on all three nodes; the only diffs versus before-prepare are the expected boot-identity and systemd-private tmp directory changes from the required restarts.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9721148416724fae\",\"cmd-f4ac28fe30d848dc\",\"cmd-e7d2f54776fa4f67\",\"cmd-0b61b53a44cd4032\",\"cmd-4f97dd9bb6f84d01\",\"cmd-d620550c218d41de\",\"cmd-8dc7b82c3aee4936\",\"cmd-15d4adefc7364b8a\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-service